worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{x\\pi }{2})=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x\\pi }{2} & =m\\pi  \\\\ x & =2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite.Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nA={…,-2,0,2,4,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Since $A$ is infinite, and $A⊆B,$ it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊆C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,$ $B,$ and $C.$\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d"} {"question" "q-181674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({sec}^{2}⁡x)=2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,{sec}^{2}⁡x,2{sec}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is also infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x)=-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)=-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x,-27\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nC={3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=4,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $B$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181652" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d"} {"question" "q-181652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181652" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ and $B$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{3x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x+1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})=-{e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{e}^{-x})={e}^{-x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-x})=-{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{-x},-{e}^{-x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{3x})=3{e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{e}^{3x})=9{e}^{3x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{3x})=27{e}^{3x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{3x},3{e}^{3x},9{e}^{3x},27{e}^{3x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x+1})={e}^{x+1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x+1}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181638" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d"} {"question" "q-181638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"] ["text" "None of the sets are infinite."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181638" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\sqrt{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x)=2 \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2)=0 \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>3,$ we have ${f}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nA={{x}^{2},2x,2,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Similar to the previous case, listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\sqrt{x})=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}})=-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}})=\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\sqrt{x},\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}},-\\frac{1}{4\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\frac{3}{8\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181599" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\frac{1}{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({x}^{3})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(4x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})=-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA={\\frac{1}{x},-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={x}^{3} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})=3{x}^{2} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})=6x \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6x)=6 \\\\ {g}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6)=0 \\\\ {g}^{(5)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>4,$ we have ${g}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nB={{x}^{3},3{x}^{2},6x,6,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=5,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =4x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x)=4 \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4)=0 \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(0)=0 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, for $n>2,$ we have ${h}^{(n)}(x)=0.$ Listing a few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\nC={4x,4,0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C|=3,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181702" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d"} {"question" "q-181702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181702" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A$ only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x-1})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{-2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x/2})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x-1})={e}^{x-1} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x-1}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{-2x})=-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{-2x})=4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{-2x})=-8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={{e}^{-2x},-2{e}^{-2x},4{e}^{-2x},-8{e}^{-2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2})=\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x/2},\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{x/2},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e146cf3342d1a448b3bcc321380e2d6f73a85d06a1d9ec18bdd36ec307186b3d"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$B$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "q-181643" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$B$ and $C$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/state.json" "file_sha256" "26868adcc116d61737d586bbaecaf2c4690e611538023e87d1c8c8ceaeae86ce"} {"question" "e-13171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13171.json" "file_sha256" "4364622df985db78c7b21ffe860d847440809770338f45f6ec9318d820a6458e"} {"question" "e-13171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13171.json" "file_sha256" "4364622df985db78c7b21ffe860d847440809770338f45f6ec9318d820a6458e"} {"question" "e-13174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: : \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\: : \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n\n- $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\: : \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13174.json" "file_sha256" "d3d04daa0535db6995dbcb148973ebf092a19603c7aba1dc36b534e03e847739"} {"question" "e-13174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation $\\sin (x\\pi) = 1$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}+2m,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A| = 0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nB = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13174.json" "file_sha256" "d3d04daa0535db6995dbcb148973ebf092a19603c7aba1dc36b534e03e847739"} {"question" "e-13175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n\n- $C =  \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3})  \\: : \\:  n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13175.json" "file_sha256" "6c8ed700bc87a8a3b2fe3832976ba1669a43ee423bb9676a60505b95da910b57"} {"question" "e-13175" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{5x})=5{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{5x})=25{e}^{5x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(25{e}^{5x})=125{e}^{5x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nA = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-6{e}^{-x})=6{e}^{-x} \\\\ {g}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{-x})=-6{e}^{-x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n\n$$\nB = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 2,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x+3})=2{e}^{x+3} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nC = \\{ 2e^{x+3} \\}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|C| = 1,$ which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/example-13175.json" "file_sha256" "6c8ed700bc87a8a3b2fe3832976ba1669a43ee423bb9676a60505b95da910b57"} {"question" "q-181722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$A,B,$ and $C$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "568ba7274cfdbdc23cad0c6794b9b90a65f1f6dfa744fd0405041e224d1a6585" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Every integer n>=3 satisfies |2n|>5, so A contains infinitely many elements. Those same integers are rational and real, so A is a subset of both B and C, making all three sets infinite. Original option 3 is therefore correct. Stored '$A, B$, and $C$' has identical meaning. This is a source-backed derived judgment corroborated by the authentic worked solution and successful grading." "bindings" {"problem" "bca568b8993e73d573c79a6deee39fb4b29e2453d199602c08035b4e0a9b0de9" "worked_solution" "046a69f38dd3ad6025a4080469a9706ef73fd6da1efbeee89b320c1b742fdc3f" "original_field" "2b7d8a0f37162f8ceabe9eb8568e534e5213c5f62b72b0a0c59d5cd5d861f0b3" "reviewed_field" "69115af384980db628f93dfdcb21d2acb8be527f4dd4fe85a5df3eb388aa7991" "raw_widget" "891313d636fe2d8cb85ac160d8b5805dbbf1fab1618a03394036db7b9d258426"} "evidence_files" [{"path" "q-181722-before.png" "sha256" "60bf8dfdc385e32ca9dd60616e40d0cd9aad25a5e36b8ed5b8d31b23c4bfe24a"} {"path" "q-181722-after.json" "sha256" "fd2e53bd099c8c40af70b0c0e3079d4126e741d898288f4a0e1c6f343216f8f1"} {"path" "history-q-181722.png" "sha256" "4f3281b67d395d46b92e6a090939323ba5605e677e03081d757149abfd158a02"}]} {"question" "q-181722" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$A$ and $B$ only"] ["text" "$A$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$A,B,$ and $C$"] ["text" "$B$ and $C$ only"] ["text" "$C$ only"]] "category" "reviewed_ma_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14040284/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "568ba7274cfdbdc23cad0c6794b9b90a65f1f6dfa744fd0405041e224d1a6585" "rationale" "Every integer n>=3 satisfies |2n|>5, so A contains infinitely many elements. Those same integers are rational and real, so A is a subset of both B and C, making all three sets infinite. Original option 3 is therefore correct. Stored '$A, B$, and $C$' has identical meaning. This is a source-backed derived judgment corroborated by the authentic worked solution and successful grading."}] "usage" {"q-181597" [] "q-181668" [] "e-13171" [] "q-181599" [] "e-13175" [] "e-13174" [] "q-181536" [] "q-181722" []} "basis" 1423 "retractions" [[17592186198724 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\mid 2n \\mid > 5}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\mid 2q \\mid > 5}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\mid 2x \\mid > 5}$"] [17592186198724 :question/answer-fields 17592186195348] [17592186198711 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: n^{2} - 5n + 6 > 0}$\n2. $B = {m ∈ Z: m^{2} - 5m + 6 < 0}$\n3. $C = {q ∈ Q: q^{2} - 5q + 6 < 0}$"] [17592186198713 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\sin (2nπ) = 1}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\sin (2qπ) = 1}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\sin (2xπ) = 1}$"] [17592186198719 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {n ∈ Z: \\cos n = 0}$\n2. $B = {q ∈ Q: \\cos q = 0}$\n3. $C = {x ∈ R: \\cos x = 0}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians."] [17592186198714 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = {f^{(n)}(x) = \\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}(x^{2}): n ∈ N_{0}}$\n2. $B = {g^{(n)}(x) = \\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}(\\ln x): n ∈ N_{0}}$\n3. $C = {h^{(n)}(x) = \\frac{d^{n}}{dx^{n}}(\\sqrt{x}): n ∈ N_{0}}$"] [17592186198084 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: n^2-9 \\geq 0 \\}$\n2. $B = \\{m \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: m^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$\n3. $C = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\:: \\: q^2-9 \\leq 0 \\}$"] [17592186198084 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R$.\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 9 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)(x + 3) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3$, and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4386/Source/Images/1312340fd7cc5c013bf4edfc5b131205.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is\n$x \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3$\nand can be expressed as $[MATH: x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).]$\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is\n$-3 \\leq x \\leq 3$\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3]$.\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1$. Therefore,\n$A = \\{\\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{3, 4, \\ldots \\}$.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$, the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2$. Therefore,\n$B = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}$.\nConsequently, $\\mid B \\mid = 7$, which is finite.\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2$. Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\""] [17592186198085 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\{n \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\:: \\: \\sin (n\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n2. $B = \\{q \\in \\mathbb{Q} \\:: \\: \\sin (q\\pi) = 1 \\}$\n3. $C = \\{x \\in \\mathbb{R} \\:: \\: \\sin (x\\pi) = 1 \\}$"] [17592186198085 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by finding the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n\\sin (x\\pi) = 1\n$$\n\nfor $x\\in\\mathbb R{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nxπ &= \\frac{π}{2} + 2mπ \\\\\nx &= \\frac{1}{2} + 2m, m ∈ Z\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in\\mathbb Z$ such that $\\dfrac{1}{2} + 2m \\in\\mathbb Z$. Therefore,\n$A = \\{\\}$.\nConsequently, $\\mid A \\mid = 0$, which is finite.\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $B$ for different values of $m$, we have\n$B = \\left\\{\\ldots,-\\dfrac{3}{2},\\dfrac12, \\dfrac{5}{2}, \\dfrac{9}{2},\\ldots\\right\\}$.\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B \\subseteq C$, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\""] [17592186198086 :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n1. $A = \\left\\{f^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(e^{5x}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n2. $B = \\left\\{g^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(6e^{-x}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$\n3. $C = \\left\\{h^{(n)}(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d^n}{\\textrm d x^n}(2e^{x+3}) \\:: \\: n\\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\right\\}$"] [17592186198086 :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$f^{(0)}(x)|= e^{5x}; f^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{5x}) = 5e^{5x}; f^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(5e^{5x}) = 25e^{5x}; f^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(25e^{5x}) = 125e^{5x}; ⋮$\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n$A = \\{e^{5x}, 5e^{5x}, 25e^{5x}, 125e^{5x}, \\ldots\\}$\nConsequently, $A$ is infinite.\n- The set $B$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$g^{(0)}(x)|= 6e^{-x}; g^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(6e^{-x}) =-6e^{-x}; g^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(-6e^{-x}) = 6e^{-x}; g^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(6e^{-x}) =-6e^{-x}; ⋮$\nThus, $B$ contains only two elements:\n$B = \\{6e^{-x}, -6e^{-x}\\}$\nConsequently, $\\mid B \\mid = 2$, which is finite.\n- The set $C$ is also finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n$h^{(0)}(x)|= 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(1)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(2)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; h^{(3)}(x)|= \\frac{d}{dx}(2e^{x + 3}) = 2e^{x + 3}; ⋮$\nThus, $C$ contains only one element:\n$C = \\{2e^{x+3} \\}$\nConsequently, $\\mid C \\mid = 1$, which is finite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [17592186195266 17592186195267 17592186195268 17592186195269 17592186195270 17592186195278 17592186195279 17592186195280 17592186195281 17592186195282 17592186195284 17592186195285 17592186195286 17592186195287 17592186195288 17592186195319 17592186195320 17592186195321 17592186195322 17592186195323 17592186195349 17592186195350 17592186195351 17592186195352 17592186195353] "immutable_fields" [[17592186195265 :answer-field/correct] [17592186195265 :answer-field/id] [17592186195265 :answer-field/key] [17592186195265 :answer-field/type] [17592186195277 :answer-field/correct] [17592186195277 :answer-field/id] [17592186195277 :answer-field/key] [17592186195277 :answer-field/type] [17592186195283 :answer-field/correct] [17592186195283 :answer-field/id] [17592186195283 :answer-field/key] [17592186195283 :answer-field/type] [17592186195318 :answer-field/correct] [17592186195318 :answer-field/id] [17592186195318 :answer-field/key] [17592186195318 :answer-field/type] [17592186195348 :answer-field/correct] [17592186195348 :answer-field/id] [17592186195348 :answer-field/key] [17592186195348 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
